Day 02 of my bug bounty series: how the web actually works.

Yesterday was rules and setup. Today I went back to the part most beginners skip.

You can't break what you don't understand. Every bug I'll ever find lives inside a request or a response.

So today I learned 4 things:

1. HTTP basics: browsers and servers talk through requests and responses. Burp Suite just lets me see that conversation.
2. Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE. Each one has a purpose, and using the wrong one in the wrong place is where bugs hide.
3. Status codes: 200, 301, 403, 404, 500. A 403 and a 404 tell you very different things about what's behind a page.
4. Headers and cookies: this is how sites remember who you are. Sessions, logins and authentication all run through here.

Nobody posts about HTTP. But every serious vulnerability starts with reading a request properly.

